<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spanish conquest of Iberian Navarre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1512">1512</a>–<a href="/years/1524">1524</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spanish conquest of Iberian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> was the invasion of the Kingdom of Navarre south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a>, beginning in 1512. Navarrese and French attempts to restore the native monarchs, John III and Catherine, failed, notably at Noáin (1521), and the fall of Amaiur and Fuenterrabía by 1524 left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-navarre">Upper Navarre</a> to the Spanish crown, while Lower Navarre stayed independent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spanish-conquest-of-iberian-navarre.jpg" alt="Spanish conquest of Iberian Navarre" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miguillen · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conquista_de_Navarra.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Since the mid-15th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> had been torn by the War of the Bands between the noble factions of the Beaumonts and the Agramonts, which left it open to its neighbours: Castilian troops took the Sonsierra in 1461, and from 1476 the kingdom was a de facto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. After Isabella's death in 1504 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a> married Germaine of Foix, daughter of a claimant to the Navarrese throne. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1511">Holy League</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 4 August 1511, Navarre declared itself neutral, and on 18 July 1512 its envoys concluded the Fourth Treaty of Blois with Louis XII for mutual help in keeping that neutrality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The next day Ferdinand's troops under Don Fadrique de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a>, crossed from Álava, and by late August 1512 almost all Iberian Navarre was in Spanish hands. Castilian forces then burned and plundered villages of Lower Navarre. A counterattack by John III with 15,000 Navarrese, Gascons and landsknechts in October failed to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>. In March 1513 a Parliament of Navarre reduced to the Beaumont party accepted Ferdinand as king, and he promised to keep the kingdom's institutions and its law, the fueros. To justify the conquest he commissioned the jurists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-nebrija">Antonio de Nebrija</a> and Juan López de Palacios Rubios, and relied on the papal bull Pastor Ille Caelestis of 21 July 1512.</p><p>A small expedition in 1516 was stopped at Roncevaux, and in 1517 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-jimenez-de-cisneros">Cardinal Cisneros</a>, regent for Charles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, ordered every Navarrese castle pulled down. In 1521, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> was distracted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-comuneros">Revolt of the Comuneros</a>, Henry II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a> sent an army of 12,000 infantry under General Asparros; once the comuneros had been crushed at Villalar, a Castilian army of 30,000 drove it back. The last resistance ended with the surrender of Hondarribia in 1524, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> issued a general pardon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Spanish troops withdrew from Lower Navarre in 1528, and the Treaty of Cambrai of 1529 confirmed the division along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. Confiscated lands of Henry II's supporters were returned only slowly, amid trials of Agramont leaders, and distrust of Navarrese loyalty lasted through the 16th century while a Protestant, independent Navarre survived to the north until its absorption into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1620. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-navarre">Upper Navarre</a> lost its status as a kingdom at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-carlist-war">First Carlist War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spanish conquest of Iberian Navarre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
